    "teacherReflection": "1. Did learners' measurements give ratios close enough to 0.70, 0.57 and 0.82 for them to see the pattern, and how did they explain small differences?\n2. Which learners predicted that the ratio would change, and did the data change their thinking? What evidence persuaded them?\n3. Could learners match sine, cosine and tangent to the correct pair of sides? Which pairs were confused most often?\n4. How well did learners link the flagpole and building ratios (5.6/8.0 and 70.0/100) to the idea that the angle fixes the shape?\n5. What would you change in the measuring routine (roles, time, precision) to make the Observe Phase run more smoothly next time?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We measured the sides of three right-angled triangles (A, B and C) with the same 35 degree angle but different sizes. In all three, opposite/adjacent was 0.70, opposite/hypotenuse was about 0.57 and adjacent/hypotenuse was about 0.82, with only small differences caused by measurement error.",
      "learned": "For a fixed acute angle, the ratios of the sides of a right-angled triangle are constant whatever the size. sin theta = opposite / hypotenuse, cos theta = adjacent / hypotenuse, tan theta = opposite / adjacent.",
      "explained": "The flagpole (5.6 m high, 8.0 m shadow) and Wanjiru's building (70.0 m above eye level at 100 m) both give opposite/adjacent = 0.70 because both triangles have a 35 degree angle. The angle fixes the shape, so it fixes the ratios, and a ratio such as tan can link a known side to an unknown side."
    }
