      "purpose": "Learners read sin, cos and tan of acute angles from four-figure tables and a scientific calculator in DEG mode and check them against the ratios they measured from triangles A, B and C in Lesson 2, so that tan 35 degrees = 0.7002 becomes a trusted tool for the surveyor.",
      "knowledge": "• Four-figure mathematical tables and a scientific calculator (in DEG mode) both give sin, cos and tan of an acute angle to 4 decimal places.\n• For 35 degrees: sin 35 = 0.5736, cos 35 = 0.8192, tan 35 = 0.7002.\n• Ratios measured from drawn triangles (opp/adj = 0.70, opp/hyp about 0.57, adj/hyp about 0.82) agree with the table values to about 2 decimal places; the small differences are measurement error.\n• Table values for 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 70 and 80 degrees can be read and recorded in a class table.",
      "skills": "• Reading a trigonometric table row and column accurately and recording values to 4 d.p.\n• Using a scientific calculator in DEG mode to find sin, cos and tan of a given angle.\n• Comparing measured and tabulated values and explaining differences as measurement error (NGSS practice: analysing and interpreting data).\n• Working cooperatively in groups of 4 or 5 to share the work and check one another.",
      "attitudes": "• Curiosity and care about accuracy when reading tables and checking calculator settings.\n• Willingness to trust evidence from two independent sources only after comparing them.\n• Respect for each member's contribution in group work.",
