    "overview": "Mr Otieno reminds the class of the surveyor's puzzle: Wanjiru Kamau reads 35 degrees from 100 m and states a height, and the Mwangaza Secondary School flagpole shadow triangle (8.0 m, 35 degrees) has the same opp/adj ratio. In Lesson 2 learners measured triangles A, B and C and found opp/adj = 0.70, opp/hyp about 0.57 and adj/hyp about 0.82. Today they ask: where does a surveyor get these ratios without drawing triangles?\n\nLearners first predict whether the table and calculator values for 35 degrees will match their measured values, and how close. In groups they then read sin, cos and tan of 35 degrees from four-figure tables, repeat with a scientific calculator set to DEG mode, and compare both with their measured ratios from triangles A, B and C. They then split the angles 10, 20, 40, 50, 70 and 80 degrees among group members and record values in a class table, noting that tan 80 = 5.6713 is much larger than tan 10 = 0.1763.\n\nIn the Explain phase learners reason about why the measured ratios differ slightly (pencil thickness, reading a ruler to the nearest mm) while the tabulated values do not, and why the table can be trusted. The lesson closes by adding new questions to the DQB and revising the class model: angle -> ratio from table or calculator -> unknown length. Complementary angles and relationships among the ratios are left for Lessons 4 and 5.",
