        "learnerExperience": "Learners write the pattern in words ('the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement') and in symbols (sin θ = cos(90° − θ), cos θ = sin(90° − θ)). To explain why, each group draws a right-angled triangle with angles 35° and 55° and labels the sides relative to 35° and then relative to 55°. They see that the side opposite 35° is the side adjacent to 55°. They write sin 35° = opposite/hypotenuse = cos 55°, and cos 35° = adjacent/hypotenuse = sin 55°. They then use the relationship to predict cos 25° from sin 65° without a calculator, checking with the calculator afterwards.",
        "teacherMoves": "Ask: 'Why do you think sin 35° and cos 55° are equal? Use the triangle, not the table.' WAIT TIME: 15 seconds. Say: 'Label opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse from the 35° corner in one colour, then from the 55° corner in another. What changes? What stays the same?' Cold-call 3 groups to explain the swap of opposite and adjacent, and prompt: 'Which side is the hypotenuse for both angles?' Pose: 'If sin 65° = 0.9063 from tables, what is cos 25°?' Ask learners to say why. Tell the class that the table values are 4 d.p. approximations of exact ratios.",
