      "purposeInStoryline": "Lessons 2 to 4 gave the definitions, the table of values and the complement pattern. Lesson 5 connects the three ratios to each other (tan = sin ÷ cos) and shows how they behave as the angle grows. This explains why Wanjiru Kamau's multiplier tan 35° = 0.7002 is a single number that depends only on the angle, and prepares learners for the exact special angles (Lesson 6) and the clinometer work (Lesson 7).",
      "safetyNotes": "No specific hazards. Learners use tables, calculators and paper only."
